

===== STATUS: success =====
Question: Before 2019, what model architectures were proposed for language understanding?

Before 2019, several influential model architectures were proposed for language understanding. The Transformer architecture emerged as a foundational model for this domain (devlin2018bertpretrainingof pages 10-12). ELMo introduced deep contextualized word representations, advancing beyond static embeddings (devlin2018bertpretrainingof pages 10-12). GPT demonstrated the effectiveness of unsupervised learning for language understanding through a left-to-right pre-trained language model approach (devlin2018bertpretrainingof pages 10-12, lewis2020retrievalaugmentedgenerationfor pages 7-9).

Earlier embedding-based approaches included GloVe word embeddings and Skip-thought vectors, which provided distributed word representations (devlin2018bertpretrainingof pages 10-12). Bidirectional LSTM models were also developed during this period (devlin2018bertpretrainingof pages 10-12). Additionally, attention mechanisms and semi-supervised sequence learning approaches contributed to the development of modern language understanding systems (devlin2018bertpretrainingof pages 10-12).

BERT, introduced in October 2018, generalized previous findings by employing deep bidirectional Transformers, representing a significant advancement over unidirectional architectures (devlin2018bertpretrainingof pages 9-10). These pre-trained models could be applied to downstream tasks through either fine-tuning or feature-based approaches, with fine-tuning becoming the dominant paradigm (devlin2018bertpretrainingof pages 9-10). For certain tasks like Named Entity Recognition, task-specific architectures such as BiLSTM classifiers were layered on top of contextual embeddings (devlin2018bertpretrainingof pages 9-10).

